window.YD_BUNDLE = {"research/01-who-gets-to-leave-home.md": "---\ntitle: \"Who Gets to *Leave Home?*\"\nslug: who-gets-to-leave-home\ntype: research\nnumber: 1\nseries: \"Research #01\"\ndate: 2026-10-13\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Cities\", \"London\", \"Mumbai\"]\ndescription: \"The global struggle for affordable youth housing — and what different countries are doing about it.\"\nread: \"14 min\"\nart: house\nstat:\n  value: \"3.4\"\n  unit: \"bn\"\n  label: \"people worldwide lack access to secure, safe and adequate housing\"\n  source: \"UN-Habitat, World Cities Report 2026\"\n---\n\n<!-- Text reproduced verbatim from YD01.docx. Charts and tables are added as visual aids;\n      every figure in them is either quoted in the paper or comes from the cited source. -->\n\n<div class=\"abstract-box\">\n<span class=\"label o\">Abstract</span>\n<p class=\"abstract-text\">Urbanization is on the rise all over the world, under the premise that economic and educational opportunities are heavily concentrated in global financial capitals. However, access to these opportunities is increasingly guarded by a worldwide cost of living crisis, the most impacted and impacting sector of which is housing. This paper investigates youth housing precarity using a comparative analysis of two global urban hubs: London, representing a Western Liberal Market Economy, and Mumbai, representing a rapidly urbanizing landscape in the East. Assessing what the barriers to entry are to the city, we also attempt to answer the highly political question of “who gets to leave the nest and who is bound to stay”. Our purposive interviews will shed light on the lived experience of young people navigating hurdles imposed on them from above, they never had a say on. This paper aims to bridge the gap between policy-makers and policy-takers, empowering the latter to take up space in decision processes that are not designed to include them.</p>\n</div>\n\n## Introduction\n\nToday, we are witnessing a world that produces unprecedented economic, educational, and professional opportunities, most of which are heavily concentrated in cities. Urbanization continues to accelerate worldwide, fed by the assumption that city-living automatically translates into broader prosperity and upward mobility for an increasingly large pool of people. While these urban engines do generate vast opportunities, a critical policy question remains: who, in fact, has access to these bubbles of prosperity? In other words, who gets to leave home, and gets access to those opportunities?\n\nTo understand the demographics of those who actually benefit from the modern city, we must scrutinize the steep barriers to entry that guard these urban hubs. Among the multiple hurdles, shelter is the most prominent one.\n\nAccording to UN-Habitat's *World Cities Report*, up to 3.4 billion people globally lack access to secure, safe, and adequate housing. The crisis is particularly acute in the rental sector: UN-Habitat defines a housing cost burden as spending more than 30% of income on rent (with severe burden exceeding 50%), and globally, 44% of rental households spend over 30% of their income just to keep a roof over their heads.\n\nSimilarly, OECD data reveals that across member countries, roughly one in three low-income tenant households spends over 40% of their disposable income on rent. This stress hits early-career entrants hardest: UN-Habitat highlights that over 70% of young people are unable to own housing in the cities where they live. This is driven by a combination of rising living costs, precarious employment, and facility undersupply.\n\nThis is a global issue with common structural denominators (ranging from financialization and speculative real estate markets to regulatory gridlock) all over the world, though the specific administrative mechanisms, cultural expectations, and market dynamics that lock young people out of housing look very different depending on geography.\n\nHence, in order to analyze the phenomenon and investigate how differently it affects young people based on the urban context they experience, the paper will focus on London and Mumbai as case studies. Despite their geographical and regulatory differences, London and Mumbai are deeply comparable in that they both operate as global economic and financial capitals and educational magnets that attract vast inflows of young talent. The two case studies provide good enough external validity for the geographical area they represent. London-related findings will be analytically generalizable (with the due contextual differences) to other similar liberalized market economies across the Western side of the globe. The same will be valid to Mumbai-related findings to other rapidly urbanizing Asian megacities.\n\nThe findings of the paper will be supported by macrodata related to the case studies we are analyzing and by two semi-structured qualitative interviews. Interviews have been conducted on one experiential subject for London and one key informed observer for Mumbai, based on purposive sampling. Far from being representative, the sample size (N=2) allows carrying out exploratory research, to identify the primary causes of the youth housing crises.\n\n## Visible cost of leaving, hidden cost of staying\n\nThe cost-of-living crisis paired with precarious employment and growing urbanization results in increasing hurdles for youngsters to own in the cities where they live. This narrows youth’s scope of decision to two options:\n\n- having to spend over 40% of disposable income on rent\n- or staying in their parental house\n\nOption 1 is the reality for 10% of young people across the EU, while Option 2 remains the defining reality for the majority of young people, with OECD and Eurostat data confirming that more than half of young people in their twenties live with their parents across most developed nations, a figure that climbs to roughly three-quarters in Southern Europe.\n\n{{ figure cost/compare }}\n\nThe extent to which this can be considered a choice is debatable, if we take into account that staying in the parental home is often less of a lifestyle preference than a constraint imposed by unaffordable rents and insecure incomes. Recent research on residential trajectories highlights how deeply family economic background shapes young people’s housing conditions. A phenomenon that Tammaru et al. (2026) call “residential echo”: the socio‑economic status of the neighbourhoods in which children end up as adults closely mirrors both their childhood residential circumstances and their parents’ socio‑economic position. Many different factors feed this path dependence, such as direct financial support for deposit, rent and the transmission of economic and cultural capital through education and employment. Ultimately, choosing to rent independently in a different city is a privilege, rather than free will.\n\nTherefore, all the young people that cannot count on such privilege, will be unable to take up the best opportunities offered for employment and education, if they happen to be located in cities where housing is unaffordable. With cities generating over 80% of global GDP, the increasing urbanization pattern of economic activity that we witness all over the world seems here to stay, as predicted by the World Bank Group and UN DESA. This will reduce employment opportunities outside of the urban landscape even further, narrowing down job prospects for youngsters who cannot afford moving out. Additionally, a lack of access to secure and affordable housing also has long-term demographic consequences. The longer young people stay in their parental home, the further they would delay family formation, or the fewer children they are going to have. This makes the housing policy issue one that cannot be left untackled by national and international policy-makers, increasingly concerned with ageing population and shrinking workforce. Finally, on a more individual level, the 2025 Eurofound survey shows that unchosen permanence in parental houses is also linked with poor young people’s mental health and family conflicts.\n\n{{ figure independence/gender }}\n\n## Case study comparisons\n\nIn order to unpack these global housing trends, we will rely on two main case studies: the cities of London and Mumbai. Those are broadly representative of their respective geographical location, with London representing the West, with widespread financialization of the housing sector and Mumbai standing for the East, where informal housing arrangements and family networks dictate the housing landscape. With this simplification, we are not dismissing the internal variety existing in those geographical areas, but the aim of our paper is to isolate the hurdles faced by youngsters in Western Liberal Market Economies (LMEs) and Eastern rapidly (and often informally) urbanizing landscape. We will attempt to bring forth the lived experiences of two youngsters navigating these hurdles, in the hopes of giving their voice visibility and due attention.\n\n## The London living scene\n\nThe youth housing crisis is particularly acute in London, considering that this city is significantly younger than the rest of the country, with about a third (33%) of residents aged 20 to 39 at the 2021 Census (Greater London Authority, 2022). London is also highly international: 40.6% of residents were born outside the UK, and 23.3% hold a non-UK passport, meaning more than half of foreign-born Londoners are not UK citizens (Office for National Statistics, 2022). Hence, our decision to interview an international university student as one representative of a very popular demographic in London.\n\n```chart\ntype: bars\ntitle: \"London: a young, international city\"\nsubtitle: \"Share of London residents, 2021 Census\"\nunit: \"%\"\ndecimals: 1\nseries:\n  - name: \"Share of residents\"\n    color: ref\nrows:\n  - [\"Aged 20 to 39\", 33]\n  - [\"Born outside the UK\", 40.6]\n  - [\"Hold a non-UK passport\", 23.3]\nsource: \"Greater London Authority (2022); Office for National Statistics (2022), Census 2021\"\nnote: \"“Aged 20 to 39” is given as “about a third (33%)” in the source.\"\n```\n\nThe renting scene in London is extremely expensive and unaffordable to students and young workers. One of the main reasons is the guarantor trap. Most London landlords and agents require a UK-based guarantor, particularly for students and international renters who lack a UK credit history or verifiable UK income. English Housing Survey data reports that 22% of private renters across England were asked to provide a guarantor before moving in, a figure standard for students and first-time renters in competitive London. Generation Rent (2024) notes that 44% of recent private renters were asked for a guarantor and 20% had to pay rent upfront. Our respondent reported that the need for a UK guarantor was ultimately the reason why she preferred a university accommodation, over a private rent. In her account, she reported the systemic difficulty encountered by international students in finding one:\n\n```chart\ntype: bars\ntitle: \"The guarantor trap\"\nsubtitle: \"Share of private renters asked for a guarantor, or to pay rent upfront\"\nunit: \"%\"\ndecimals: 0\nseries:\n  - name: \"Share of renters\"\n    color: young\nrows:\n  - [\"Guarantor — all private renters, England\", 22]\n  - [\"Guarantor — recent private renters\", 44]\n  - [\"Rent paid upfront — recent renters\", 20]\nsource: \"English Housing Survey; Generation Rent (2024)\"\nnote: \"The two surveys cover different groups of renters, so the bars are not directly comparable with each other.\"\n```\n\n> \"While British students can rely on their parents, broader family or any acquaintance to guarantee for them, it is more unlikely for non British students to have a British network instead. This means relying on and profusely paying for agencies that guarantee for them... The need for expensive agencies is ultimately what made me settle for a university accommodation.\"\n\nWithout a UK guarantor, international students often rely on university-backed schemes or paid guarantor services ranging from £40 a month to one month's rent. Those costs tend to inflate renting prices, making living expenses even more burdensome. Until recently, renters who could not provide a UK guarantor, such as international students, were commonly asked to pay 6 to 12 months' rent in advance instead. Eventually, under the 2025 Renters’ Rights Act, landlords are banned from taking rent before a tenancy is entered into and can require no more than one month’s rent in advance after signing. While this curbs abusive advance demands, it makes the lack of a UK-based guarantor an even more essential pre-requisite for London renters, even harder to circumvent to international students or workers.\n\nAnother closely linked issue is London Transport cost. Transport for London (TfL) operates one of the most expensive (and extensive) public transit networks in the world. While full-time students living in London can apply for an 18+ Student Oyster photocard to secure a 30% discount on season tickets (Travelcards and Bus and Tram Passes), this discount is restrictive to the specific zone the student resides in and pays for. It does not apply to individual pay-as-you-go (PAYG) single fares, which are very common in students’ daily lives, especially if they have part-time work commitments all across London. Therefore, even if students are willing to compromise on prime living locations, the money saved up on rent would be completely swallowed by commuting expenses. As our respondent reported:\n\n> “In terms of trade-offs, I would have been willing to trade commuting time with some cheaper rent, and while that would have been possible, transport is so expensive to the point where living in central London or in the outskirts would have cost roughly the same.\"\n\nTo bypass per-trip metro costs (where single rides regularly exceed £2), students substitute public transit with walking, which directly alters their daily schedules. As our respondent noted:\n\n> “speaking of transport, I take transport only when I am in a rush for classes. Otherwise I prefer walking which saves up lots of money... in order to stick to my plan not to use transport, I will have to sacrifice a bit of sleep (for a 9 am class I will have to leave at 7:30).\"\n\n## Mumbai Living Scene\n\nIn Mumbai, about 25% of city renters end up spending over 50% of their monthly income. According to CREDAI-MCHI (2024), the average annual rent for a one-bedroom (1BHK) apartment in Mumbai is ₹5.18 lakh, which exceeds the average annual salary of a junior professional (₹4.49 lakh). Our interviewee was very outspoken about Mumbai renting unaffordability:\n\n```chart\ntype: bars\ntitle: \"A year’s rent costs more than a year’s pay\"\nsubtitle: \"Mumbai, ₹ lakh per year\"\nunit: \" lakh\"\ndecimals: 2\nseries:\n  - name: \"₹ lakh per year\"\n    color: young\nrows:\n  - [\"Average rent, 1BHK\", 5.18]\n  - [\"Average junior salary\", 4.49]\nsource: \"CREDAI-MCHI (2024)\"\n```\n\n```chart\ntype: bars\ntitle: \"How much of their income Mumbai renters spend on rent\"\nsubtitle: \"Share of Mumbai renters, by share of income spent on rent\"\nunit: \"%\"\ndecimals: 0\nseries:\n  - name: \"Share of renters\"\n    color: young\nrows:\n  - [\"Over 50% of income\", 25]\n  - [\"41–50% of income\", 15]\nsource: \"NoBroker Rent Report 2026\"\n```\n\n> “I live with my parents, because it was impossible to find an accommodation. It is a 15 minutes commute from my house to college... There are many advantages of living with family: it saves time and money. In India the education system is very tough and there is no time for other stuff… There is not even time to cook for yourself. It’s very common for people to live with family in Mumbai. But there are also disadvantages, like the fact that you cannot learn to live independently. At my age (23) you are supposed to live independently, have a part time job and sustain yourself, but the situation in India does not allow you to do that.”\n\nHostel arrangements are widely opted for by entry-level workers, and can range from ₹10,000 to ₹75,000 per month, though the sector is largely unverified: of 1,483 hostels and PGs listed on Magicbricks, only 149 were verified (Free Press Journal, 2026). At the 2011 Census, the most recent available, 41% of Mumbai households lived in slums. While hostel arrangements are common all over India, according to our interviewee, Mumbai seems to be the exception:\n\n> “While hostel arrangements are common all over India for students, in Mumbai it is not the case. In Bangalore, my friends live in hostels and are more independent, because student hostels are cheaper and offer better living conditions.”\n\nUnlike London, Mumbai's transit system is financially accessible via local suburban rail networks, but it takes a severe mental and physical toll on its roughly 7.5 million daily passengers: between January 2014 and May 2025, 29,970 people died and 30,214 were injured on the suburban network, mostly from falling off overcrowded trains or crossing tracks (Press Trust of India, 2025). Commuters nonetheless seem willing to pay that cost in exchange for lower rents.\n\n> “Transport is the only option... So people choose travel over living in a place that is close by to work. Community is not a problem and is not expensive at all. Local trains are how most people travel and it is very packed. People fight trying to get on the train. There is no other choice.”\n\nUnaffordable rent ends up turning into a deterrent, pushing students and workers out of Mumbai entirely and redirecting them to other cities, financially more within reach:\n\n> \"One of my friends decided not to study in Mumbai, but in Bangalore, because she had to live alone and living alone is just impossible in Mumbai, not financially feasible.\"\n\n| Two cities at a glance | London | Mumbai |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main barrier to renting | UK guarantor requirement | Rent above junior salaries |\n| Advance rent | Max. one month after signing (Renters’ Rights Act 2025) | — |\n| Transport | Single rides regularly exceed £2 | Suburban rail affordable, but overcrowded |\n| Common youth housing | University accommodation | Living with family; hostels and PGs |\n| Hostels and PGs | — | ₹10,000–₹75,000 a month; 149 of 1,483 listings verified |\n\n## Policy requests from London and Mumbai\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to bring forth lived experiences of young people facing hurdles that they have not contributed to shaping. Far from being a comprehensive policy recommendation, this section of the paper aims to give visibility to those voices that our decision-making system too often silences. So, the following are the policy recommendations that our respondents would like to see implemented in the context they are living in:\n\n- Policy Recommendation for the Greater London Authority (GLA): the GLA, together with higher education providers, should establish a city-backed municipal guarantor scheme. Since international renters already have to undergo the financial screening procedure imposed by the Home Office, as testified by the Visa Status Share Code, landlords should take this as sufficient proof of risk mitigation. This scheme would directly resolve the structural trap that disproportionately impacts international and non-local youth.\n\n  Additionally, Transport for London (TfL) should extend the 18+ Student Oyster photocard discount framework to include Pay-As-You-Go (PAYG) single peak and off-peak travel across all zones.\n\n- Policy Recommendations for the Maharashtra State Housing Department: formalize paying guest facility businesses, implementing a regulatory framework for private youth hostels, introducing mandatory registration and municipal inspections.\n\n  Additionally, municipal authorities should incentivize public-private partnerships to spark investment in affordable student residences.\n\n## Bibliography:\n\n<div class=\"bib\">\n\n- Anarock Property Consultants. (2026). *Residential real estate market report: Mumbai Metropolitan Region (MMR)*. Anarock Research. [https://www.anarock.com/research-insights](https://www.anarock.com/research-insights)\n- CREDAI-MCHI. (2024, October 18). *Mumbai residential rental study and youth compensation gaps* [https://www.livemint.com/news/india/brain-drain-in-mumbai-due-to-ever-rising-real-estate-prices-people-earn-rs-4-49-lakh-to-pay-rs-5-18-lakh-for-re-11729244633576.html](https://www.livemint.com/news/india/brain-drain-in-mumbai-due-to-ever-rising-real-estate-prices-people-earn-rs-4-49-lakh-to-pay-rs-5-18-lakh-for-re-11729244633576.html)\n- Eurofound. (n.d.). *European housing dashboard*. European Foundation for the Improvement of Living and Working Conditions. [https://www.eurofound.europa.eu/en/surveys-and-data/data-catalogue/housing-dashboard](https://www.eurofound.europa.eu/en/surveys-and-data/data-catalogue/housing-dashboard)\n- Eurostat. (2025). *Young people – housing conditions* (Statistics Explained). European Commission. [https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Young_people_-_housing_conditions](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/statistics-explained/index.php?title=Young_people_-_housing_conditions)\n- Free Press Journal. (2026, May 29). *Mumbai’s women face housing crisis amid rising demand for safe student hostels*. Free Press Journal. [https://www.freepressjournal.in/education/mumbais-women-face-housing-crisis-amid-rising-demand-for-safe-student-hostels](https://www.freepressjournal.in/education/mumbais-women-face-housing-crisis-amid-rising-demand-for-safe-student-hostels)\n- Greater London Authority. (2022). *2021 Census first release: Population and households*. GLA City Intelligence. [https://data.london.gov.uk/download/2021-census-first-release/18e4a444-11a7-4777-b383-8cf199176157/2021%2520census%2520first%2520release.pdf](https://data.london.gov.uk/download/2021-census-first-release/18e4a444-11a7-4777-b383-8cf199176157/2021%2520census%2520first%2520release.pdf)\n- Hyland, M., et al. (2025). *Foundational challenges: The housing struggles of Europe’s youth*. Eurofound. [https://doi.org/10.2806/9785435](https://doi.org/10.2806/9785435)\n- Office for National Statistics. (2022, November 2). *International migration, England and Wales: Census 2021*. ONS. [https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/populationandmigration/internationalmigration/bulletins/internationalmigrationenglandandwales/census2021](https://www.ons.gov.uk/peoplepopulationandcommunity/populationandmigration/internationalmigration/bulletins/internationalmigrationenglandandwales/census2021)\n- Press Trust of India. (2025, June 10). *Nearly 30,000 deaths on Mumbai suburban train network in 11 years: Police*. The Week. [https://www.theweek.in/wire-updates/national/2025/06/10/bom29-mh-train-deaths.html](https://www.theweek.in/wire-updates/national/2025/06/10/bom29-mh-train-deaths.html)\n- UK Council for International Student Affairs. (n.d.). *Accommodation for international students*. UKCISA. [https://www.ukcisa.org.uk/student-advice/life-in-the-uk/accommodation/](https://www.ukcisa.org.uk/student-advice/life-in-the-uk/accommodation/)\n- UN-Habitat. (2026). *World Cities Report 2026: The global housing crisis: Pathways to action*. United Nations Human Settlements Programme. [https://unhabitat.org/world-cities-report-2026](https://unhabitat.org/world-cities-report-2026)\n- United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs, Population Division. (2025). *World urbanization prospects: Summary of results*. United Nations. [https://www.un.org/development/desa/pd/world-urbanization-prospects-2025](https://www.un.org/development/desa/pd/world-urbanization-prospects-2025)\n- Wadhwa, D., & Laesser, C. (2026). Building productive cities for an urbanizing world. In A. F. Pirlea, D. Wadhwa, D. Mahler, U. Serajuddin, M. Welch, A. Thudt, & M. Lambrechts (Eds.), *Atlas of Global Development 2026*. World Bank Group. [https://data360.worldbank.org/en/atlas/urban-development](https://data360.worldbank.org/en/atlas/urban-development)\n- World Bank Group. (2026). *Urban development overview: Cities as engines of growth*. World Bank. [https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/urbandevelopment/overview](https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/urbandevelopment/overview)\n\n</div>\n", "data-lab/01-age-of-independence.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Age of Independence\"\nslug: age-of-independence\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 1\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Europe\", \"Gender\"]\ndescription: \"At what age do young Europeans leave the parental home — and how much does it differ between countries, over time, and between women and men?\"\ndataset: independence\nart: house\n---\n\n{{ answer independence }}\n\n{{ figure independence/gender }}\n\n{{ figure independence/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Eurostat estimates the average age at which young people leave the parental household from the EU Labour Force Survey, using the share of young people living with their parents at each age.\n- The indicator is an estimate of a typical age, not an observed average of individual moves.\n", "data-lab/02-cost-of-being-young.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Cost of Being Young\"\nslug: cost-of-being-young\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 2\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Housing\", \"Europe\", \"Cost of living\"]\ndescription: \"How many young people spend more than 40% of their income just on housing — and how does that compare with everyone else?\"\ndataset: cost\nart: rooms\n---\n\n{{ answer cost }}\n\n{{ figure cost/compare }}\n\n{{ figure cost/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Housing cost overburden rate: the share of people living in households where total housing costs (net of housing allowances) represent more than 40% of disposable income (net of housing allowances).\n- Eurostat does not publish harmonised cross-country data on other living costs (food, transport) broken down by the age of the person, so this post measures housing costs only.\n", "data-lab/03-first-paycheck.md": "---\ntitle: \"The First Paycheck\"\nslug: first-paycheck\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 3\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Work\", \"Europe\", \"Wages\"]\ndescription: \"How much do workers under 30 earn per hour across Europe once prices are taken into account — and how far behind the average are they?\"\ndataset: paycheck\nart: ladder\n---\n\n{{ answer paycheck }}\n\n{{ figure paycheck/compare }}\n\n{{ figure paycheck/ratio }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- The Structure of Earnings Survey (SES) is run every four years; 2022 is the latest wave. It covers enterprises with at least 10 employees and excludes public administration (NACE section O).\n- Mean gross hourly earnings include overtime pay. PPS is an artificial currency that equalises purchasing power across countries.\n", "data-lab/04-where-young-people-are-going.md": "---\ntitle: \"Where Young People Are Going\"\nslug: where-young-people-are-going\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 4\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Mobility\", \"Europe\", \"Migration\"]\ndescription: \"At what age do people move between countries — and which European countries gain or lose young adults through migration?\"\ndataset: migration\nart: globe\n---\n\n{{ answer migration }}\n\n{{ figure migration/age }}\n\n{{ figure migration/net }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Immigration and emigration count people changing their usual residence for at least 12 months, including nationals returning or leaving.\n- Emigration is typically harder to record than immigration (people do not always deregister), so net figures may overstate inflows. Some countries record age in completed years and others as age reached during the year; each country is used under the definition it reports.\n", "data-lab/05-youth-jobs-map.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Youth Jobs Map\"\nslug: youth-jobs-map\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 5\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Work\", \"Global\", \"Employment\"]\ndescription: \"Where in the world do young people find work — and where are they locked out of jobs, education and training?\"\ndataset: jobs\nart: grid\n---\n\n{{ answer jobs }}\n\n{{ figure jobs/map }}\n\n{{ figure jobs/trend }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Unemployment and participation are ILO modelled estimates, which fill gaps in national data to produce comparable series for all countries. Recent years are estimates and are revised as survey data arrive.\n- Youth unemployment is measured against the labour force (those working or looking for work), not the whole youth population; students not seeking work are outside the labour force. NEET is measured against the whole population aged 15–24.\n", "data-lab/06-generation-gap.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Generation Gap\"\nslug: generation-gap\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 6\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Europe\", \"Poverty\", \"Income\"]\ndescription: \"Are young adults or older people more likely to be poor — and how does their income compare?\"\ndataset: gap\nart: queue\n---\n\n{{ answer gap }}\n\n{{ figure gap/poverty }}\n\n{{ figure gap/trend }}\n\n{{ figure gap/income }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- At-risk-of-poverty rate: share of people whose equivalised disposable income (after social transfers) is below 60% of the national median. It measures low income relative to others in the same country, not absolute deprivation.\n- Income refers to the household a person lives in; many 18–24-year-olds still live with their parents and share their household income.\n", "data-lab/07-education-to-work.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Education-to-Work Journey\"\nslug: education-to-work\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 7\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Education\", \"Europe\", \"Employment\"]\ndescription: \"How likely are young people to be in work one to three years after leaving education — and how much does the kind of education matter?\"\ndataset: eduwork\nart: book\n---\n\n{{ answer eduwork }}\n\n{{ figure eduwork/levels }}\n\n{{ figure eduwork/vocational }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- The indicator covers 20–34-year-olds who are no longer in education or training and who completed their highest level of education one to three years earlier.\n- Eurostat flags some estimates as having low reliability (small samples); these are shown faded and marked in the table.\n", "data-lab/08-a-day-in-the-life.md": "---\ntitle: \"A Day in the Life\"\nslug: a-day-in-the-life\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 8\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Europe\", \"Time use\"]\ndescription: \"How do 15–24-year-olds spend their 24 hours — and how does their day differ from everyone else’s?\"\ndataset: day\nart: clock\n---\n\n{{ answer day }}\n\n{{ figure day/24h }}\n\n{{ figure day/diff }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Time-use surveys ask respondents to keep a diary of their activities in 10-minute slots. Figures are averages over all respondents in the group and over all days of the week.\n- The 2020 wave of the Harmonised European Time Use Surveys (HETUS) covers the countries shown; fieldwork dates differ by country.\n", "data-lab/09-youth-population-map.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Youth Population Map\"\nslug: youth-population-map\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 9\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Society\", \"Global\", \"Demography\"]\ndescription: \"Where is the population of 15–24-year-olds growing, and where is it shrinking, between now and 2050?\"\ndataset: population\nart: town\n---\n\n{{ answer population }}\n\n{{ figure population/map }}\n\n{{ figure population/regions }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Population aged 15–24 is the sum of the male and female 15–19 and 20–24 age groups. Projections are the UN World Population Prospects medium variant, as published by the World Bank.\n- Projections are not forecasts: they depend on assumptions about fertility, mortality and migration.\n", "data-lab/10-digital-generation.md": "---\ntitle: \"The Digital Generation\"\nslug: digital-generation\ntype: data-lab\nnumber: 10\ndate: 2026-09-23\ntags: [\"Technology\", \"Europe\", \"AI\", \"Digital skills\"]\ndescription: \"How many young people use generative AI and have basic digital skills — and how far ahead of older adults are they?\"\ndataset: digital\nart: phone\n---\n\n{{ answer digital }}\n\n{{ figure digital/ai }}\n\n{{ figure digital/purposes }}\n\n{{ figure digital/skills }}\n\n## About the data\n\n- Data come from the EU survey on ICT usage in households and by individuals. “All” refers to individuals aged 16–74.\n- Overall digital skills combine five areas (information, communication, content creation, safety and problem solving); “basic or above” means at least basic in all five.\n", "resources/01-finding-youth-data.md": "---\ntitle: \"Finding Youth *Data*\"\nslug: finding-youth-data\ntype: resources\nnumber: 1\nseries: \"Guide\"\ndate: 2026-09-25\ntags: [\"Data\", \"Guide\", \"Open statistics\"]\ndescription: \"A beginner’s guide to the open databases behind most youth statistics — where to look, what to check, and how to cite what you find.\"\nread: \"7 min\"\nart: grid\nauthor: \"Youth Dividend Data Lab\"\n---\n\nMost numbers about young people’s lives — unemployment, housing costs, education, migration — come from a handful of official, free databases. You don’t need special access to use them. This guide shows where to start, and what to check before you quote a figure.\n\n## Where to look\n\n- **[Eurostat](https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat)** — the statistical office of the European Union. The best source for comparing EU countries, and many EFTA and candidate countries. Its youth indicators are gathered under the theme “Youth”.\n- **[ILOSTAT](https://ilostat.ilo.org)** — the International Labour Organization’s database. Global data on employment, unemployment and young people not in employment, education or training (NEET).\n- **[World Bank Open Data](https://data.worldbank.org)** — global indicators for almost every country, including ILO labour estimates and UN population figures, all downloadable.\n- **[OECD Data Explorer](https://data-explorer.oecd.org)** — detailed comparisons across OECD members on education, wages, housing and social policy.\n- **[UN World Population Prospects](https://population.un.org/wpp/)** — population estimates and projections by age for every country.\n- **National statistical offices** — the most detailed source for a single country, and usually where the international figures come from in the first place.\n\n## How to cite it\n\nGive the organisation, the indicator or dataset code, the year of the data and the date you retrieved it. For example:\n\n> Eurostat, *Estimated average age of young people leaving the parental household* (yth_demo_030), 2025. Retrieved 25 September 2026.\n\nEvery chart in our [Data Lab](#/explore?format=data-lab) lists its sources this way, and offers the underlying numbers as a table and CSV download.\n\n## Going further\n\nFound a number that seems to tell an important story about young people? [Tell us](#/about?s=participate) — it may become the next Data Lab post.\n", "resources/02-youth-indicators-explained.md": "---\ntitle: \"Youth Indicators, *Explained*\"\nslug: youth-indicators-explained\ntype: resources\nnumber: 2\nseries: \"Explainer\"\ndate: 2026-09-25\ntags: [\"Data\", \"Explainer\", \"Employment\", \"Housing\"]\ndescription: \"Plain-language definitions of the six measures that appear most often in youth research — and the mistakes people make when reading them.\"\nread: \"8 min\"\nart: book\nauthor: \"Youth Dividend Data Lab\"\n---\n\nHeadlines about young people often rest on a few technical indicators. Each one measures something specific — and something narrower than it sounds. Here is what they mean, based on the official definitions used by Eurostat and the International Labour Organization.\n\n## Youth unemployment rate\n\nThe share of young people **in the labour force** who are without work, available to start, and actively looking for a job. The labour force is everyone working or looking for work.\n\n**Common mistake:** reading it as the share of *all* young people who are unemployed. Students who are not looking for work are outside the labour force, so a high rate can apply to a small group. See it mapped in [The Youth Jobs Map](#/data-lab/youth-jobs-map).\n\n## Labour-force participation rate\n\nThe share of young people who are either working or looking for work.\n\n**Common mistake:** treating a low rate as a problem in itself. It is often low because many young people are in full-time education.\n\n## NEET rate\n\nThe share of young people **not in employment, education or training**, measured against the whole population of that age.\n\n**Common mistake:** assuming every NEET young person is looking for work. The group also includes people caring for family, living with illness or disability, or simply between activities.\n\n## At-risk-of-poverty rate\n\nThe share of people whose household income, adjusted for household size, is **below 60% of the national median** after social transfers.\n\n**Common mistake:** reading it as absolute poverty. It measures low income *relative to others in the same country*, so rates are not a direct comparison of living standards between countries. See [The Generation Gap](#/data-lab/generation-gap).\n\n## Housing cost overburden rate\n\nThe share of people living in households where total housing costs take up **more than 40% of disposable income**.\n\n**Common mistake:** assuming it covers everyone who struggles with rent. People who have not moved out — or who share with others to keep costs down — may not appear in it. See [The Cost of Being Young](#/data-lab/cost-of-being-young).\n\n## Purchasing power standards (PPS)\n\nAn artificial common currency that removes differences in price levels between countries, so that incomes and wages can be compared by what they buy rather than their face value.\n\n**Common mistake:** comparing wages across countries in euros alone. A lower wage can go further where prices are lower. See [The First Paycheck](#/data-lab/first-paycheck).\n\n## One rule for all of them\n\nAlways give the age band, the year and the source. “Youth unemployment is high” means little; “the unemployment rate among 15–24-year-olds in the labour force, 2025, ILO modelled estimate” can be checked.\n"};
